1
00:00:00,065 --> 00:00:02,405
The Great Emu War (1932)

2
00:00:03,045 --> 00:00:04,825
November fourth, 1932.

3
00:00:05,545 --> 00:00:06,545
In Western Australia,

4
00:00:06,905 --> 00:00:08,205
soldiers wait beside a dam

5
00:00:08,245 --> 00:00:09,025
with machine guns.

6
00:00:09,625 --> 00:00:10,105
Ahead of them,

7
00:00:10,385 --> 00:00:11,645
around a thousand emus

8
00:00:11,725 --> 00:00:12,605
are approaching the water.

9
00:00:13,165 --> 00:00:14,185
The birds have finally

10
00:00:14,225 --> 00:00:15,405
gathered in one place.

11
00:00:16,045 --> 00:00:17,025
The soldiers let them come

12
00:00:17,085 --> 00:00:18,946
closer, then open fire.

13
00:00:19,506 --> 00:00:20,486
A few emus fall.

14
00:00:21,046 --> 00:00:22,486
Then a machine gun jams.

15
00:00:22,966 --> 00:00:23,966
The rest scatter

16
00:00:23,986 --> 00:00:24,906
across the open land.

17
00:00:25,466 --> 00:00:26,366
It was one of their best

18
00:00:26,426 --> 00:00:28,106
chances to stop a whole flock.

19
00:00:28,626 --> 00:00:30,106
Almost every bird escaped.

20
00:00:30,926 --> 00:00:31,706
How did Australia

21
00:00:31,766 --> 00:00:32,306
end up sending

22
00:00:32,346 --> 00:00:33,746
machine guns against emus?

23
00:00:34,406 --> 00:00:35,206
To understand that,

24
00:00:35,466 --> 00:00:36,286
go back two days.

25
00:00:36,906 --> 00:00:37,706
On November second,

26
00:00:37,926 --> 00:00:38,886
a small army unit

27
00:00:38,966 --> 00:00:39,326
arrives

28
00:00:39,366 --> 00:00:40,366
in the wheat-growing district

29
00:00:40,406 --> 00:00:41,006
near Campion.

30
00:00:41,486 --> 00:00:42,886
The men have two machine guns

31
00:00:43,106 --> 00:00:44,246
and orders to help local

32
00:00:44,306 --> 00:00:45,606
farmers get rid of the birds.

33
00:00:46,266 --> 00:00:46,866
Those farmers

34
00:00:46,906 --> 00:00:47,526
have been fighting

35
00:00:47,566 --> 00:00:48,726
to keep their farms alive.

36
00:00:49,366 --> 00:00:50,426
After the First World War,

37
00:00:50,786 --> 00:00:51,746
many former soldiers

38
00:00:51,786 --> 00:00:52,627
had settled on land

39
00:00:52,687 --> 00:00:53,607
in Western Australia.

40
00:00:54,167 --> 00:00:55,167
Some of that land was hard

41
00:00:55,207 --> 00:00:56,627
to farm even in good years.

42
00:00:57,187 --> 00:00:58,087
In parts of the district,

43
00:00:58,427 --> 00:00:59,847
settlers had already abandoned

44
00:00:59,867 --> 00:01:00,367
their farms.

45
00:01:00,987 --> 00:01:02,387
Then the Great Depression

46
00:01:02,407 --> 00:01:02,687
struck.

47
00:01:03,187 --> 00:01:04,367
Wheat prices collapsed.

48
00:01:04,867 --> 00:01:05,387
Drought made

49
00:01:05,607 --> 00:01:06,307
everything worse.

50
00:01:06,907 --> 00:01:07,967
For the families who stayed,

51
00:01:08,227 --> 00:01:08,767
the harvest

52
00:01:08,827 --> 00:01:09,687
was their livelihood.

53
00:01:10,207 --> 00:01:11,247
A lost field of wheat

54
00:01:11,307 --> 00:01:12,407
could mean a year of work

55
00:01:12,427 --> 00:01:13,627
with nothing to show for it.

56
00:01:14,207 --> 00:01:14,587
And now

57
00:01:14,847 --> 00:01:16,007
the emus were arriving.

58
00:01:16,567 --> 00:01:17,387
They were huge,

59
00:01:17,527 --> 00:01:19,187
flightless birds, nearly as tall

60
00:01:19,207 --> 00:01:21,007
as a person, with powerful legs

61
00:01:21,067 --> 00:01:21,707
built for running.

62
00:01:22,367 --> 00:01:23,227
Thousands were moving

63
00:01:23,247 --> 00:01:24,207
through the farming districts

64
00:01:24,447 --> 00:01:25,707
looking for food and water.

65
00:01:26,247 --> 00:01:26,808
To the birds,

66
00:01:27,068 --> 00:01:27,728
the wheat fields

67
00:01:27,788 --> 00:01:28,688
were an easy meal.

68
00:01:29,208 --> 00:01:29,828
To the farmers,

69
00:01:30,108 --> 00:01:30,668
they were crops

70
00:01:30,688 --> 00:01:31,208
that had taken

71
00:01:31,348 --> 00:01:32,368
months of work to grow.

72
00:01:32,948 --> 00:01:33,708
The emus ate

73
00:01:33,748 --> 00:01:34,968
the grain and trampled what

74
00:01:34,988 --> 00:01:35,568
they didn't eat.

75
00:01:35,968 --> 00:01:36,928
They damaged fences

76
00:01:36,968 --> 00:01:38,288
as they moved through, leaving

77
00:01:38,408 --> 00:01:39,708
openings for rabbits to enter

78
00:01:39,768 --> 00:01:41,008
and do even more harm.

79
00:01:41,528 --> 00:01:42,448
The farmers couldn't

80
00:01:42,488 --> 00:01:42,968
keep them out.

81
00:01:43,568 --> 00:01:44,368
So they turned

82
00:01:44,408 --> 00:01:45,288
to the government for help.

83
00:01:45,868 --> 00:01:46,708
The Defence Minister

84
00:01:46,768 --> 00:01:47,408
agreed to send

85
00:01:47,508 --> 00:01:48,668
soldiers with machine guns.

86
00:01:49,208 --> 00:01:49,968
A cameraman came

87
00:01:50,008 --> 00:01:51,308
along to record the operation.

88
00:01:51,888 --> 00:01:52,348
On paper,

89
00:01:52,548 --> 00:01:53,528
the plan seemed simple.

90
00:01:54,108 --> 00:01:54,968
Find a large flock.

91
00:01:55,368 --> 00:01:56,508
Bring the guns within range.

92
00:01:56,928 --> 00:01:57,488
Open fire.

93
00:01:58,068 --> 00:01:58,668
But the soldiers

94
00:01:58,708 --> 00:01:59,568
discovered the problem

95
00:01:59,748 --> 00:02:01,189
almost as soon as they arrived.

96
00:02:01,709 --> 00:02:02,449
On their first day,

97
00:02:02,649 --> 00:02:04,089
they spotted a group of emus.

98
00:02:04,689 --> 00:02:06,029
Farmers tried to herd the birds

99
00:02:06,089 --> 00:02:06,949
toward the waiting guns.

100
00:02:07,489 --> 00:02:09,209
Instead, the flock broke apart.

101
00:02:09,749 --> 00:02:10,829
Some birds ran one way.

102
00:02:11,229 --> 00:02:12,029
Others ran another.

103
00:02:12,529 --> 00:02:12,989
By the time

104
00:02:13,009 --> 00:02:13,929
the soldiers could shoot,

105
00:02:14,149 --> 00:02:15,429
there were only small groups

106
00:02:15,509 --> 00:02:16,489
moving across the fields.

107
00:02:17,049 --> 00:02:18,109
Only a few were killed.

108
00:02:18,749 --> 00:02:20,049
The soldiers had weapons made

109
00:02:20,069 --> 00:02:21,469
to deliver sustained fire.

110
00:02:21,929 --> 00:02:22,489
But that meant

111
00:02:22,609 --> 00:02:23,509
little when the targets

112
00:02:23,569 --> 00:02:24,289
kept scattering.

113
00:02:24,829 --> 00:02:25,629
And then came

114
00:02:25,649 --> 00:02:27,069
the opportunity at the dam.

115
00:02:27,589 --> 00:02:28,849
Around a thousand emus

116
00:02:28,889 --> 00:02:29,949
were gathered near the water.

117
00:02:30,529 --> 00:02:32,009
This time the soldiers waited

118
00:02:32,069 --> 00:02:32,949
until they were close

119
00:02:33,009 --> 00:02:33,669
before firing.

120
00:02:34,169 --> 00:02:35,590
But after roughly a dozen birds

121
00:02:35,630 --> 00:02:37,270
were killed, a gun jammed.

122
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:38,690
The flock escaped.

123
00:02:39,190 --> 00:02:40,350
The men had found the large

124
00:02:40,410 --> 00:02:41,190
target they needed.

125
00:02:41,690 --> 00:02:42,270
And lost it.

126
00:02:42,910 --> 00:02:43,570
So they tried

127
00:02:43,610 --> 00:02:44,210
something different.

128
00:02:44,670 --> 00:02:45,610
They put a machine gun

129
00:02:45,690 --> 00:02:46,590
on the back of a truck.

130
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:48,050
If they couldn't get the birds

131
00:02:48,090 --> 00:02:48,910
to come to the guns,

132
00:02:49,190 --> 00:02:50,030
they could take the gun

133
00:02:50,170 --> 00:02:50,790
after the birds.

134
00:02:51,330 --> 00:02:52,530
There was just one problem.

135
00:02:53,090 --> 00:02:54,490
Emus can run fast.

136
00:02:54,970 --> 00:02:55,730
The truck couldn't

137
00:02:55,770 --> 00:02:56,950
keep up over the rough ground.

138
00:02:57,410 --> 00:02:57,890
It jolted

139
00:02:58,030 --> 00:02:59,230
so badly that the gunner

140
00:02:59,290 --> 00:03:00,030
struggled to aim.

141
00:03:00,570 --> 00:03:01,210
A machine gun

142
00:03:01,250 --> 00:03:02,010
was now bouncing

143
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:03,690
across the countryside, chasing

144
00:03:03,730 --> 00:03:05,070
birds it could barely reach.

145
00:03:05,650 --> 00:03:06,930
The soldiers kept trying.

146
00:03:07,390 --> 00:03:08,250
But each encounter

147
00:03:08,330 --> 00:03:09,430
ended much the same way:

148
00:03:09,910 --> 00:03:11,511
a burst of gunfire, birds

149
00:03:11,571 --> 00:03:13,131
scattering, and more emus

150
00:03:13,231 --> 00:03:14,451
disappearing over the fields.

151
00:03:14,971 --> 00:03:15,611
Their commander,

152
00:03:15,791 --> 00:03:17,111
Major Meredith, compared

153
00:03:17,131 --> 00:03:18,411
the birds' ability to withstand

154
00:03:18,491 --> 00:03:19,971
gunfire to that of tanks.

155
00:03:20,571 --> 00:03:21,311
Back in Canberra,

156
00:03:21,531 --> 00:03:22,731
the operation was attracting

157
00:03:22,831 --> 00:03:23,991
a different kind of attention.

158
00:03:24,491 --> 00:03:25,571
Politicians questioned

159
00:03:25,591 --> 00:03:26,151
the campaign.

160
00:03:26,751 --> 00:03:27,891
One joked that if anyone

161
00:03:27,911 --> 00:03:28,591
deserved medals,

162
00:03:28,851 --> 00:03:29,571
it was the emus.

163
00:03:30,151 --> 00:03:31,371
A few days after the failed

164
00:03:31,471 --> 00:03:32,531
ambush, the government

165
00:03:32,591 --> 00:03:33,511
recalled the soldiers.

166
00:03:34,071 --> 00:03:34,631
In Canberra,

167
00:03:34,831 --> 00:03:35,951
the shooting was over.

168
00:03:36,451 --> 00:03:37,331
In the farming district,

169
00:03:37,571 --> 00:03:37,971
the birds

170
00:03:38,031 --> 00:03:39,051
were still eating wheat.

171
00:03:39,551 --> 00:03:40,491
The soldiers had gone,

172
00:03:40,731 --> 00:03:42,131
but the damage hadn't stopped.

173
00:03:42,711 --> 00:03:44,211
Fences were still being broken.

174
00:03:44,612 --> 00:03:45,832
Crops were still at risk.

175
00:03:46,312 --> 00:03:47,692
The birds were still moving

176
00:03:47,712 --> 00:03:48,352
through the district.

177
00:03:48,872 --> 00:03:49,372
The farmers

178
00:03:49,552 --> 00:03:50,452
asked for help again.

179
00:03:50,912 --> 00:03:51,932
And the government agreed

180
00:03:51,972 --> 00:03:52,732
to another attempt.

181
00:03:53,292 --> 00:03:54,152
So the soldiers

182
00:03:54,232 --> 00:03:55,212
returned with their guns.

183
00:03:55,792 --> 00:03:56,252
This time,

184
00:03:56,492 --> 00:03:57,732
they killed more emus.

185
00:03:58,232 --> 00:03:58,752
But the work

186
00:03:58,872 --> 00:04:00,052
dragged on for weeks.

187
00:04:00,612 --> 00:04:01,432
The birds were spread

188
00:04:01,472 --> 00:04:02,732
across too much country,

189
00:04:02,972 --> 00:04:04,092
and the soldiers had to find

190
00:04:04,112 --> 00:04:05,072
them before they could fire.

191
00:04:05,632 --> 00:04:06,252
Even when the guns

192
00:04:06,292 --> 00:04:06,972
were successful,

193
00:04:07,272 --> 00:04:08,312
another group of emus

194
00:04:08,352 --> 00:04:09,392
could turn up somewhere else.

195
00:04:09,972 --> 00:04:11,312
By December, hundreds

196
00:04:11,352 --> 00:04:12,272
of birds had been killed.

197
00:04:12,812 --> 00:04:14,112
Even so, thousands

198
00:04:14,172 --> 00:04:15,072
remained in the district.

199
00:04:15,532 --> 00:04:16,472
The shooting hadn't

200
00:04:16,552 --> 00:04:17,312
stopped the damage.

201
00:04:17,812 --> 00:04:19,293
The army ended its campaign.

202
00:04:19,813 --> 00:04:20,453
For the farmers,

203
00:04:20,653 --> 00:04:21,993
there was no dramatic finish.

204
00:04:22,453 --> 00:04:23,453
They still needed ways

205
00:04:23,493 --> 00:04:24,453
to protect their fields.

206
00:04:25,013 --> 00:04:25,973
In the years that followed,

207
00:04:26,233 --> 00:04:27,333
people continued killing

208
00:04:27,413 --> 00:04:28,513
emus for bounty payments,

209
00:04:28,833 --> 00:04:29,693
while fences remained

210
00:04:29,733 --> 00:04:31,053
important for protecting crops.

211
00:04:31,633 --> 00:04:32,213
For the birds,

212
00:04:32,473 --> 00:04:33,993
the campaign had been deadly.

213
00:04:34,593 --> 00:04:35,453
Many had been shot.

214
00:04:35,893 --> 00:04:37,033
But thousands remained

215
00:04:37,073 --> 00:04:37,793
across the region.

216
00:04:38,073 --> 00:04:38,953
Australia had sent

217
00:04:39,033 --> 00:04:40,193
soldiers into farmland

218
00:04:40,213 --> 00:04:41,073
with machine guns

219
00:04:41,173 --> 00:04:42,013
to stop the birds

220
00:04:42,033 --> 00:04:43,073
from destroying the wheat.

221
00:04:43,633 --> 00:04:45,353
The soldiers had fired, chased,

222
00:04:45,573 --> 00:04:46,893
withdrawn, and returned.

223
00:04:47,393 --> 00:04:47,913
The farmers

224
00:04:48,073 --> 00:04:49,133
still needed protection.

225
00:04:49,653 --> 00:04:50,693
The emus had suffered

226
00:04:50,833 --> 00:04:51,493
heavy losses.

227
00:04:52,073 --> 00:04:53,573
Yet across the wheat-growing

228
00:04:53,613 --> 00:04:55,214
district, the birds continued

229
00:04:55,274 --> 00:04:56,494
searching for food and water.

230
00:04:57,014 --> 00:04:58,014
The soldiers were gone.

231
00:04:58,534 --> 00:04:59,934
And the emus kept coming.

